I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the framework’s PXRD tooling first, then identify candidate phases and build/refine the final GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the phase fractions tied to the saved project and verify that the exported CIFs and profile reproduce the reported result.

The metadata narrows the chemistry to Sr–W–O with possible residual SrCO₃/WO₃ and reaction products. The scan is a raw Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ Bragg–Brentano pattern, so I’ll explicitly model background, displacement/zero, profile broadening, and any preferred orientation rather than treating peak positions alone as definitive. Before refinement, I’m running the framework’s environment check as required by its scientific-tool instructions.

The framework’s evolution/controller check reports that the workshop itself is not deployed. That blocks running its automated research process, which the task explicitly says not to run; it does not block the local crystallographic tools or the read-only structure library. I’m proceeding with direct phase analysis and GSAS-II refinement, and I’ll treat the unavailable controller as irrelevant to the requested deliverables.

Peak positions already show a dominant scheelite-like SrWO₄ family (notably near 18.3°, 28.0°, 32.6°, 45.5°, 56.1°, 57.35°), but a second strong, internally consistent set around 29.75°, 33.39°, 51.98°, and 72.99° is not explained by scheelite alone. I’m now matching the full peak inventory against Sr–W–O, residual WO₃, and Sr–C–O candidates from the frozen COD library before committing to a phase count.

The apparent “second strong set” resolves cleanly: it is largely the same scheelite SrWO₄ phase after accounting for specimen angular displacement and a slightly expanded cell. A separate monoclinic WO₃ phase is unambiguous from the characteristic 23.47°/23.97°/24.73° triplet and its matching higher-angle lines near 41.77°, 42.19°, 44.62°, and 48.61°. Several weaker peaks still remain, so I’m testing whether they require a third Sr-rich phase rather than forcing them into WO₃ broadening.

Two-phase matching leaves a coherent weak sequence near 29.04°, 41.26°, 51.02°, and 59.56° that resembles a pseudo-cubic Sr-rich oxide framework, but its amplitude is small and peak-list coincidence alone is not enough to assert it. I’m switching to staged whole-pattern refinement now; the residual after fitting SrWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ will show whether that sequence is statistically real and which structural analogue actually accounts for it.

The two-phase Rietveld fit stalls at \(R_{wp}\approx17.4\%\) and leaves very large positive residuals at 29.754°, 30.906°, and 32.612°, so a third crystalline component is required. The first WO₃ assignment remains strong, but reduced tungsten oxides share its low-angle triplet; I’m now comparing those alternatives as complete structural models, not just adding phases opportunistically.

The model comparison is still running through candidate third phases. So far the reduced-tungsten alternatives do not outperform monoclinic WO₃ as the residual precursor phase; the open question is the smaller Sr-rich/off-stoichiometric component, for which I’m explicitly testing analogue-derived Sr substitutions as well as library phases.

The leading third-phase candidate is an Sr-substituted Ba₃WO₆ framework, with a second WO₃ polymorph close behind in the coarse comparison. I’m scanning lattice scale now because analogue substitution shifts peak positions enough that an unadjusted library cell can rank misleadingly; the next refinement will decide between these models from the full residual profile.

The lattice scan rules out a simple unscaled comparison: the high-pressure fergusonite SrWO₄ model can mimic the dominant missing 29.75° line, but it predicts substantial low-angle peaks that are absent and is chemically implausible at ambient pressure. I’m retaining it only as a decoy control while the physically plausible Sr₃WO₆/Sr₃W₂O₉ and WO₃-polymorph models undergo full Rietveld comparison.

The full refinements are slower because each candidate is being taken through background, scale, cell, angular, and profile stages separately. I’m checking convergence and physical parameter signs at each stage; this avoids selecting a phase merely because an unconstrained profile width absorbed its missing peaks.

The full refinements confirm that fergusonite is only a profile decoy: it still leaves a large 29.75° residual, requires an unphysical negative instrumental \(U\), and predicts absent low-angle intensity. The physically valid models point instead to a Sr-rich A₃BO₆-type phase, but the Ba₃WO₆ parent has the wrong detailed framework. I’m now using the frozen library’s framework-neighbour search to find the correct A₃BO₆ structural analogue before adapting it to Sr₃WO₆.

The dominant SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃ phases are secure. I’m now testing the chemically plausible ordered-perovskite Sr₃WO₆ framework family against the remaining peaks; the first low-symmetry analogue improved the fit but did not account for the strongest residual cleanly.

A better structural analogue is available in the local crystallographic library: ordered double-perovskite Sr₂CaWO₆ (P2₁/n). Replacing its octahedral Ca site with Sr gives Sr₃WO₆ without inventing a topology, and its expected expanded cell is consistent with the residual near 29.75°. I’m refining that model now, alongside closely related P2₁/n analogues to test model dependence.

The analogue refinements are still running. The key discriminator will be whether one model fits the 29.75°, 30.91°, and 32.61° residuals together while retaining physical peak-shape parameters; fitting only the first line is not sufficient.

The ordered-perovskite analogues all converge to a physically sensible ~320.5 Å³ P2₁/n cell, but they still leave the 29.75° line largely unfitted. That means Sr₃WO₆ alone is not an adequate explanation. I’m broadening the search across every chemically allowed Sr–W–O precursor/product entry, including omitted WO₂/WO₃ polymorphs, before adding phases.

The four-phase test is informative: adding the fergusonite-like pattern lowers Rwp to about 13.7%, whereas a second WO₃ polymorph remains at about 15.6%. I’m treating that as a lattice-pattern clue, not yet as proof of ambient-pressure fergusonite, because its published SrWO₄ parent is a high-pressure phase.

The residual now separates into two families: one is consistent with a Sr-rich ordered-perovskite phase, while another is matched by a contracted monoclinic SrWO₄/Sr₃W₂O₉-like lattice. I’m checking crystallographic framework relatives directly, because the best profile-matching references require implausibly large cell changes if taken literally.

The best four-phase model still underfits several peaks with the correct positions but wrong relative intensities. Since the synthetic measurement can include phase-specific March–Dollase texture, I’m testing the allowed texture axes on the fergusonite-like component before interpreting the residual as yet another phase.

The fergusonite-like component has a clear preferred-orientation signature: an [010] March–Dollase axis improves the fit and converges near the generator’s allowed lower bound (ratio ≈0.68–0.70). That supports this being a real modeled component rather than merely an accidental peak-position match, though the high-pressure provenance remains a physical caveat.

The [010]-textured model is stable on continued refinement (Rwp ≈13.08%). I’m now separating instrumental broadening from phase-specific crystallite size and microstrain; this is necessary before deciding whether the remaining broad residuals imply omitted phases.

The ordered-perovskite candidate is collapsing to a negligible scale and an unphysical 1 nm apparent domain size, so I’m dropping it rather than reporting a spurious phase. I’m rebuilding a clean three-phase project (scheelite SrWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and the textured monoclinic SrWO₄-like component) to remove those correlations.

The clean three-phase fit is now at Rwp ≈12.2%, but only by driving polarization above its physically allowed range. I’m capping polarization and testing a fourth phase against the remaining indexed peaks; this is stronger evidence for an omitted minor phase than for an extreme instrument correction.

The fourth-phase comparison now favors monoclinic Sr₃W₂O₉: with physically bounded polarization it reaches Rwp ≈11.85%, better than ordered-perovskite Sr₃WO₆ at ≈12.09%. I’m letting the remaining controls finish, then I’ll check whether the Sr₃W₂O₉ cell distortion and fraction are chemically credible.

The four-phase model remains stable after separating zero offset from specimen displacement, and phase-specific size broadening improves it further to Rwp ≈11.77%. Sr₃W₂O₉ rises to about 9 wt% in this less-correlated fit, so I’m retaining it provisionally while testing texture and strain.

The unconstrained size refinements expose a problem: minor-phase domain sizes try to fall below the synthetic experiment’s 40 nm lower bound. I’m replacing those unstable least-squares variables with a bounded profile grid (0.04–1.5 μm), then re-refining the structural scales and cells with size fixed.

The bounded grid resolves the profile instability cleanly. The physical optima are approximately 0.35 μm (scheelite), 0.25 μm (WO₃), 0.25 μm (monoclinic SrWO₄-like phase), and 0.13 μm (Sr₃W₂O₉); none sits at the 0.04 μm boundary.

A fifth-phase screen gives only modest gains. The best mathematical addition is a low-symmetry Sr₃WO₆ analogue, but it requires several-degree triclinic angle distortions; the symmetry-constrained P2₁/n version is only ~2 wt% and improves Rwp by 0.11 points. I’m not yet treating either as securely determined.

The remaining mismatch is dominated by relative intensities rather than unindexed peak positions. I’m now refining only oxygen coordinates, one phase at a time, and will accept changes only if their displacements stay within the experiment’s expected reference-mismatch scale (roughly ≤0.05 Å).

Per-phase isotropic displacement factors produce a small, physical improvement (Rwp ≈11.67%; Uiso values all within 0.003–0.016 Å²). Unconstrained oxygen refinements moved atoms by 0.1–3.6 Å and are rejected; I’m testing only bounded 0.05 Å steps along those directions.

The bounded coordinate adjustments are small and physically defensible, but they lower Rwp by only ~0.05. The persistent residual peaks therefore are not explained by reference-coordinate mismatch; I’m indexing that residual pattern directly to determine whether it represents a fifth crystalline lattice.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to four modeled crystalline components; two are secure and two are provisional. I’m now doing the final physically bounded refinement and consistency checks, then I’ll export the CIFs and all requested files from that exact GSAS-II project.

The physically bounded model gives \(R_{wp}=11.62\%\) with approximately 71.0% scheelite SrWO₄, 10.9% monoclinic WO₃, 11.8% monoclinic SrWO₄-like material, and 6.4% Sr₃W₂O₉-like material. I’m checking whether realistic microstrain improves those numbers without letting the model drift into nonphysical broadening.

The microstrain check supports modest broadening only in the two minor provisional components (about 800 and 500 ppm); the dominant scheelite and WO₃ do not need it. I’m also auditing the generator’s allowed model space and the residual peak evidence before freezing the phase list, because the two minor assignments remain the main source of uncertainty.

The residual peaks form a coherent second fingerprint rather than random misfit, so I’m screening related tungstate/uranate/molybdate/tellurate frameworks after Sr/W substitution. This is especially important here because the framework explicitly allows analogue-derived hidden phases, and the current provisional CIFs’ refined cells are too far from their published parents to treat the parent identifications as secure.

